# Shared Prompt: Mermaid Flowchart Conventions

This prompt defines the shared Mermaid flowchart syntax and style rules.
It is used by BOTH the Process Flow Designer (to build diagrams) and the
Flow Reviewer (to validate diagrams against the same rulebook).

## Syntax
- Always use `flowchart TD` (top-down) unless the process is naturally
  left-to-right, in which case use `flowchart LR`.
- Node types:
  - Start/End: rounded nodes — `([Start])`, `([End])`
  - Process/action step: rectangle — `[Do something]`
  - Decision point: diamond — `{Condition?}`
- Decision branches must be labeled on the edges, e.g.:
B{Payment successful?} -->|Yes| C[Confirm order] B -->|No| D[Show error message]

- Every diagram must have exactly one Start node and at least one End
node. Every path must eventually reach an End node (no dead ends).
- Use short, clear node labels (3-6 words), action-oriented
(e.g., "Validate payment", not "Payment").

## Structural rules
- Each decision node must have at least two labeled outgoing branches.
- Avoid overly long single chains — if a process has more than ~10
sequential steps, consider whether it should be split into sub-flows.
- Keep node IDs short and simple (A, B, C... or short camelCase).

## Output
Always wrap the diagram in a fenced Mermaid code block:
```mermaid
flowchart TD
  ...

---